{
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // SCOPE OF THE TYPE CHECK — READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING COVERAGE.
  //
  // `npm run typecheck` (astro check) reports 0 errors. That does NOT mean the
  // codebase type-checks. `checkJs` is FALSE, so:
  //
  //   CHECKED      src/loaders/skills.ts       (the only .ts file here)
  //                src/**/*.astro              (component script blocks)
  //
  //   NOT CHECKED — type errors in these are invisible to `astro check`:
  //                src/loaders/enumerate.mjs
  //                src/loaders/links.mjs
  //                src/loaders/markdown.mjs
  //                src/loaders/frontmatter.mjs
  //                src/loaders/site-pages.mjs
  //                src/sidebar.mjs
  //                src/site.config.mjs
  //                scripts/check-live-links.mjs
  //                scripts/prepare-assets.mjs
  //                tests/*.mjs
  //
  // That is most of the loader. Verified, not assumed: a `@type {number}`
  // annotation on a string constant, planted in enumerate.mjs and again in
  // sidebar.mjs, leaves `astro check` reporting 0 errors — while the same
  // class of error in skills.ts reports 1.
  //
  // CONSEQUENCE THAT BITES: the JSDoc `@typedef` blocks in enumerate.mjs and
  // frontmatter.mjs are INERT. They are accurate documentation, and they are
  // load-bearing for skills.ts — which imports them and IS checked, so a lie
  // in them surfaces over there — but nothing verifies them against the .mjs
  // code they sit in. Treat them as comments that happen to be
  // machine-readable, not as a guarantee.
  //
  // PHASE 2 added one file to that list: scripts/check-live-links.mjs, the
  // live-deployment link checker. Its decision-making — reference extraction,
  // internal/external classification, dist-path-to-URL mapping — is factored
  // out and unit-tested in tests/live-links.test.mjs precisely because the type
  // checker is not watching it.
  //
  // PHASE 3 added two more, and both are load-bearing:
  //
  //   src/loaders/site-pages.mjs   builds the five pages ABOUT the catalog,
  //                                including the verbatim README lifts
  //   scripts/prepare-assets.mjs   selects and copies the image assets that
  //                                SKILL.md bodies embed, before every build
  //
  // Neither is checked, and both are consumed by src/loaders/skills.ts, which
  // IS — so a shape mismatch at that boundary surfaces there. That is the only
  // type coverage they have. Their behaviour is covered by
  // tests/site-pages.test.mjs and tests/assets.test.mjs instead, for the same
  // reason the live-link checker's is.
  //
  // WHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY TURNED ON: `checkJs: true` surfaces errors that are
  // overwhelmingly missing annotations and implicit `any` under `strict` rather
  // than latent bugs. Fixing them is real work and out of scope. Logged as a
  // PHASE 5 CANDIDATE, to be sized by running the command below at the moment
  // the phase is scoped.
  //
  //   HOW TO SIZE IT (the measurement this comment used to record):
  //   flip `checkJs` to true, run `npx astro check`, restore the file. Confirm
  //   with `cmp` that the flip landed and the restore was byte-identical, and
  //   that `git status --porcelain` is empty before and after.
  //
  // NO ERROR COUNT IS RECORDED HERE, DELIBERATELY, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR A
  // DEFECT THIS COMMENT COMMITTED FOUR TIMES. A per-file census used to sit at
  // this spot. It was re-measured in Phase 2, re-measured twice in Phase 3, and
  // went stale again in the very commit whose purpose was to correct it — each
  // value correct on the day it was written and wrong by the next commit,
  // because the figure counts errors in files that every phase edits.
  //
  // The Phase 3 round-2 review is what established the pattern rather than the
  // instance. Its readings, quoted as that review's findings at 06d2a02 and
  // not as properties of this tree: 23 of 26 rows still matched, and the 3 that
  // diverged were exactly the 3 files edited after the census was taken, their
  // deltas summing to the change in the total with no residual. Attributing
  // them that way is what keeps them from becoming the next stale figure — a
  // reading indexed to a moment cannot go out of date; a reading presented as a
  // current fact can.
  //
  // A number that decays by construction is not documentation of a fact, it is
  // a copy of a measurement, and the copy is what goes false. Note also which
  // of these was the more dangerous: the total was TRUE when written, every
  // time. A false figure is catchable by anyone who reads it against the tree.
  // A true-but-unbound one fails silently — right today, nothing goes red the
  // day it stops being right. So the copy is gone and the command that produces
  // it stays.
  //
  // WHAT IS NOT DELETED: the list of unchecked files above is BOUND.
  // `tests/pins.test.mjs` R6 derives that set from the filesystem and fails if
  // any unchecked source is missing from the list block, with a non-vacuity
  // floor and a control proving the scoped search can fail. That binding is
  // measured by mutation, not asserted: deleting any one of the listed files
  // from the list block turns R6 red.
  //
  // Standard 2 says a number is re-measured at the moment it is quoted, not
  // inherited. Four recurrences here say the stronger thing: a number nothing
  // re-measures should not be written down beside a command that produces it.
  //
  // If you turn it on, do it as its own change with its own review. Do not
  // widen it as a side effect of something else, and do not restate the
  // coverage claim anywhere without changing this comment with it.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "extends": "astro/tsconfigs/strict",
  "include": [".astro/types.d.ts", "**/*"],
  "exclude": ["dist"],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": false,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true
  }
}
